Z||000000||fierospeeder||02-08-2001||12:54 PM||||I got a program that i cant get to work by using ado programmicly instead of ado controls.<P>Its a very simple.  This is for class and i can get it to work with controls but not the other way.<P>Its using a datalist and a datagrid.  You will select the item from the list and it will show the items on the datagrid that matches its partnumber.<P>thats all i need and with controls all i have to do is adcontrol.controltext="select * tblsales where fldpartnum=" & dblinventory.boundtext<P>but how do you do this without ado controls?<BR>pconinventory as new adodc.connection etc... that way.<P>i can send the program just reply back in the forum||||reg||1
Z||000001||lou_dias||02-08-2001||01:38 PM||||send me the program...<P>did you check off under:<BR>Projects<BR>    References<BR>       Microsoft ActiveX Data Objects 2.1<P>and you want to use ADODB.Connection, not ADODC.Connection I believe.<P>Just Zip it and send it to me and tell me what you want to do...||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou_dias||02-08-2001||02:10 PM||||This is how your clickevent procedure should look:<P>Private Sub dblInventory_Click()<BR>    ' You must close the table before changing it's dataset<BR>    If mrstSales.State = adStateOpen Then mrstSales.Close<BR>    <BR>    mrstSales.Source = "select * from tblsales where fldpartnum= " & dblInventory.BoundText<BR>    mrstSales.Open<BR>    Set dbgSales.DataSource = mrstSales.DataSource<BR>End Sub||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||02-08-2001||02:13 PM||||thanks im going to try it when i get home.<BR>which will be within an hour.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||02-08-2001||05:08 PM||||thank you, it does work.<P>i probably wouldn't ever get that.  My book wanted us to do the question using ADO controls.  It took me an hour setting up.  And a little more time trying to figure out the click event procedure.  The book does talk about the boundtext and using controls.<P>Our instructor later says to do it with ado but no controls.  She tried to do it in class and couldn't get it to work.  Our book does each chapter with its own examples and describes how to do it for that example.  So its harder to figure out and theres not much technical stuff to give you an idea on how it works.<P>Did it take you a while to learn ADO?  I find the other things in the book are pretty much easy, but ado has a lot things that need to be setup to work properly.  Windows programming is a lot different from pascal and C.  <P>Im going to try to setup the field names on the top columns to "partnumber" instead of "fldpartnumber"   <P>Thank you<BR>erik||||reg||1
Z||000005||lou_dias||02-09-2001||08:53 AM||||This is what I do for a living.<BR>I taught myself basic VB then I got a job and was train on Visual FoxPro 6.0 and then VB using ADO, but it was a trial and error thing that I learned on the fly.  Yeah there are alot of quirks to it that affect performance bigtime too.  Like client-side vs. server-side cursors and cursor tpyes.  VFP (Visual FoxPro) is actual more powerful from a programming point of view and it totally object oriented but is slower.  VB hides alot of details and event procedures.  That all changes with version 7.0 (.NET).  But VFP is so much better for working with database user interfaces.  VFP has ADO too but I never use it.  I use it's dynamic cursors and and SQL-Direct commands to get things done and you can put in formulas into the grid column and rows to change the colors or whatever when certain conditions exist (too highlight negative numbers for example).  Like I said, much more powerful, but slower.  I'm playing with VFP 7.0 at home and with the speed of today's machines, who cares, + 7.0 adds some nice things and all the controls are the same now.||||reg||1
